 other metabolic derangements", "Acute renal dysfunction (creatinine 1.6 mg/dL in patient with diabetes and hypertension)", "ECG findings (ST elevation) already managed as ACS, now with uncharacterised physical exam abnormality"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Cardiovascular Complications (Heart Failure / Reinfarction)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.35897435897435903, "posterior": 0.30490536749432995, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Tachycardia and hypertension likely indicate hemodynamic compromise, moderately supporting cardiovascular complications (B1) while reducing pharmacological/metabolic (B2-3) relevance."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of objective ischemic progression or congestion reduces heart failure/pathophysiological alignment in B1, but doesn't completely rule it out."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Has the patient experienced new-onset dyspnea, orthopnea, or paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea?", "Are there signs of pulmonary congestion or elevated jugular venous pressure?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Repeat ECG for new ischemic changes", "BNP or troponin testing", "Echocardiography"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.9, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiologic_pathway"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Metabolic and Post-ACS Gastrointestinal Complications", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.46153846153846156, "posterior": 0.3567896379580068, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Early post-ACS timeline doesn't clearly link to medications, moderately weakening pharmacological explanation in B3.", "Lack of urinary diagnostic data prevents confirming ATN in B4, moderately weakening this mechanism but allowing for pre-renal considerations."], "evidence_against": ["Tachycardia and hypertension likely indicate hemodynamic compromise, moderately supporting cardiovascular complications (B1) while reducing pharmacological/metabolic (B2-3) relevance.", "Insufficient procedural history weakens support for cholesterol embolism under B2 as a direct cause, creating a moderate against effect."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Was intravenous contrast administered during PCI (to evaluate cholesterol embolism)?", "Have there been episodes of diarrhea, skin nodules, or peripheral neuropathy?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Serum creatinine trend over time", "Lipid panel including LDL and triglycerides", "CT angiography for cholesterol embolism"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "systemic_pathology"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Pharmacological Side Effects and Toxicities", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.026023461119051017, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Early post-ACS timeline doesn't clearly link to medications, moderately weakening pharmacological explanation in B3."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Acute Tubular Necrosis / Post-Impairment Renal Dysfunction", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.03469794815873469, "danger": 0.65, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of urinary diagnostic data prevents confirming ATN in B4, moderately weakening this mechanism but allowing for pre-renal considerations."]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Acute Heart Failure with Acute Renal-Metabolic Worsening", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0051693462664328844, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The acute renal dysfunction (creatinine 1.6 mg/dL) combined with minimal new ECG changes weakens the case for recurrent myocardial infarction (B1.2), while absence of IV contrast during PCI weakens cholesterol embolism (B2.1). Normal oxygenation and lack of epigastric findings also reduce support for hypertriglyceridemia-induced pancreatitis (B2.2). Heart failure (B1.1) evidence remains neutral. These findings maintain metabolic/post-ACS complications (B2.1-3) as the leading candidates."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Recurrent Acute Coronary Syndrome", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.29973602122789705, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Cholesterol Embolism (Post-PCI)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.034563668173600265, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The acute renal dysfunction (creatinine 1.6 mg/dL) combined with minimal new ECG changes weakens the case for recurrent myocardial infarction (B1.2), while absence of IV contrast during PCI weakens cholesterol embolism (B2.1). Normal oxygenation and lack of epigastric findings also reduce support for hypertriglyceridemia-induced pancreatitis (B2.2). Heart failure (B1.1) evidence remains neutral. These findings maintain metabolic/post-ACS complications (B2.1-3) as the leading candidates."]}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Hypertriglyceridemia-Induced Acute Pancreatitis", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.03673652290874139, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The acute renal dysfunction (creatinine 1.6 mg/dL) combined with minimal new ECG changes weakens the case for recurrent myocardial infarction (B1.2), while absence of IV contrast during PCI weakens cholesterol embolism (B2.1). Normal oxygenation and lack of epigastric findings also reduce support for hypertriglyceridemia-induced pancreatitis (B2.2). Heart failure (B1.1) evidence remains neutral. These findings maintain metabolic/post-ACS complications (B2.1-3) as the leading candidates."]}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Ischemic Acute Pancreatitis (Post-ACS Embolic)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.28548944687566513, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 7, "bundle_size": 8, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would absence of diuretic use timeline or urine concentration metrics contradict acute tubular necrosis despite acute renal dysfunction?", "result_summary": "Lack of urinary diagnostic data prevents confirming ATN in B4, moderately weakening this mechanism but allowing for pre-renal considerations.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "moderate_for", "B3": "neutral", "B4": "moderate_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the acute renal dysfunction (creatinine 1.6 mg/dL) combined with the elevated lipase argue for acute heart failure-induced prerenal injury over pure cardiovascular complications as the primary explanation?", "result_summary": "The acute renal dysfunction (creatinine 1.6 mg/dL) combined with minimal new ECG changes weakens the case for recurrent myocardial infarction (B1.2), while absence of IV contrast during PCI weakens cholesterol embolism (B2.1). Normal oxygenation and lack of epigastric findings also reduce support for hypertriglyceridemia-induced pancreatitis (B2.2). Heart failure (B1.1) evidence remains neutral. These findings maintain metabolic/post-ACS complications (B2.1-3) as the leading candidates."}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would the absence of pulmonary edema, orthopnea, or JVP findings contradict the hypothesis of heart failure-induced renal worsening despite elevated BP and pulse?", "result_summary": "The acute renal dysfunction (creatinine 1.6 mg/dL) combined with minimal new ECG changes weakens the case for recurrent myocardial infarction (B1.2), while absence of IV contrast during PCI weakens cholesterol embolism (B2.1). Normal oxygenation and lack of epigastric findings also reduce support for hypertriglyceridemia-induced pancreatitis (B2.2). Heart failure (B1.1) evidence remains neutral. These findings maintain metabolic/post-ACS complications (B2.1-3) as the leading candidates."}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the hospital day-2 timeline with minimal new ECG findings sufficient to rule out recurrent myocardial infarction as the primary driver of the abdominal symptoms?", "result_summary": "The acute renal dysfunction (creatinine 1.6 mg/dL) combined with minimal new ECG changes weakens the case for recurrent myocardial infarction (B1.2), while absence of IV contrast during PCI weakens cholesterol embolism (B2.1). Normal oxygenation and lack of epigastric findings also reduce support for hypertriglyceridemia-induced pancreatitis (B2.2). Heart failure (B1.1) evidence remains neutral. These findings maintain metabolic/post-ACS complications (B2.1-3) as the leading candidates."}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lack of reported IV contrast administration during PCI (evident from E9) preclude cholesterol embolism as the direct cause of renal/pancreatic dysfunction?", "result_summary": "The acute renal dysfunction (creatinine 1.6 mg/dL) 